I couldn't reproduce the --print stray newlines at HEAD, so I changed no code. Following the task's honest-null clause, I committed tests that pin the exact bytes, plus a record of what I measured. The commit is 18477b87.

What I measured. I ran the real provider from /tmp, using target/debug/yoyo built at 4bf2c8a2, and piped stdout through od -c:

| Command | stdout |
|---|---|
| --print | P O N G (4 bytes) |
| --print --thinking high | P O N G |
| --print --thinking off | P O N G |
| --print with a real bash tool turn | P O N G |
| -p without --print | \n P O N G \n \n |
| piped stdin without --print | \n P O N G \n \n |

• **--print has no leak at this commit**, with thinking on, off, or with a tool call. My guess is that the assessment ran a binary built before `90e69801`, the chrome-to-stderr fix. I haven't verified that.
• **Plain -p and piped mode without --print do add newlines** (one before the answer, two after). These modes deliberately put turn framing and tool output on stdout; the reserve is never set there. So a reserve gate would not change them, and I left them alone. If you want those modes to have clean stdout too, that's a separate product decision.
• **Trailing newline: none added.** `--print` writes the model's text exactly as received, so stdout is the answer bytes and nothing else. Adding a `\n` would change output that scripts already consume.

Tests added to tests/print_stdout_contract.rs:
• A test stub that sends a thinking block before the text answer. The existing stub never did, so no test covered the switch from thinking to answer text.
• Two tests, one for -p --print and one for piped --print. Each checks that stdout starts with the answer's first byte and equals the answer exactly, using assert_eq! on untrimmed stdout.
• A check that the stub really sends the thinking event before the text.

Checks that the tests catch what they should. I ran two deliberate breakages one at a time, each restored in the same command:
• **Removing the reserve gate:** 7 of the 9 contract tests failed, including both new ones. The two plain--p tests stayed green, as they should.
• **Making `emit_output` print `"{}\n\n"`** (the doubled trailing newline the assessment described): only the two new tests failed. Every older `--print` test trims trailing whitespace before comparing, so none of them could catch this.

The reading, the reasoning and these results are in the ARCHITECTURE.md entry beside the Day 212 note on the stdout rule.

Checks: cargo build, cargo test (all green), cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo fmt -- --check all passed.
